  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 309 AM EDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\n- Additional rain mainly far south through today\n\nWe have cancelled the Flood Watch a few hours early this morning.\n\nWe have seen quite a bit longer of a break of the steady and heavier \nrain than expected Saturday afternoon through the current time. It \nseems that the area of showers/storms that moved through Saturday \nmorning, pushed the elevated baroclinic zone/front aloft a bit \nfurther south. The low level jet has taken time to re-establish \nitself, which it is now doing to our SW. \n\nIt is likely that the band of rain completely SSW of the forecast \narea early this morning could sneak up to our far SW corner as the \nnext wave pushes it north just a little bit. Consensus from new \nmodel guidance and mean amounts show that far SW Van Buren could see \nan inch or so of rain today, with lesser amounts to the NE. This \narea did not see the heavier amounts yesterday, as they were a \nlittle further north. The bottom line is we are not expecting any \nareas to see 4-5 inches total from this that was the impetus for the \nWatch.\n\nIt may take much of the day for the rain to push south of the area \nonce it moves in, but all areas should be dry by sunset tonight. \n\n- Small chance of residual showers south for Monday and Tuesday\n\nThe boundary that has been the focus of the rain the last few days \nis expected to be pushed further south, and will be much less of an \ninfluence on the area as compared to the last couple of days. \n\nThe upper low that is in charge of the synoptic pattern over the \nregion is showing signs over having less of an influence on the \nweather for Southern Lower, resulting in lower chances of rain for \nonly the southern areas through Tuesday. This trend is the result of \nthe upper low complex showing more likelihood that it rotates \nfurther south, and has much less of a direct influence on our \nweather as a stronger high pushes it south away from the area.   \n\n- Seasonable and mainly dry Wednesday and beyond\n\nThe models are in good agreement (for now) that the upper low will \nmove far enough away by Thursday that we get under the influence of a \nstronger long wave ridge that slowly builds toward the area. This \nwill get held up by stronger systems to our west and east. We will \nstart out cool with highs barely in the 60s. Building heights and a \nflow of warmer temperatures eventually from the south will push max \ntemps back up in the 70s for the latter half of the area with lows \nin the 40s and 50s.",
